00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.150 align:start position:0%
europe's massive problem of youth  unemployment tops the agenda this week  as ministers responsible for employment  social policy health and consumer  affairs meet in luxembourg  with eu leaders set next week to focus  on ways to get young people into jobs  the ministers will discuss efforts to  implement a youth guarantee scheme seen  as a top eu employment priority  on average across the 27 eu nations  almost one in four people under 25 were  out of work in april  the youth guarantee aims to ensure that  young people like them receive a quality  job offer further education an  apprenticeship or traineeship within  four months of leaving school or  becoming unemployed  youth unemployment will also be in the  spotlight as the ministers try to agree  a general approach on the european  globalization adjustment fund the egf so  that negotiations can begin with  european parliament the fund was set up  in response to the economic crisis and  helps workers who lost their jobs due to  the recession and globalization to find  new employment and develop new skills  the ministers will debate the scope of  the fund ways to finance it categories  of people who might be eligible and  support for youth employment  the conditions of those most in need  will also be on the agenda as the  council hears a progress report on the  fund for european aid to the most  deprived  eu leaders confirmed in february that  2.5 billion euros would be earmarked for  this group for the  2014-2020 period  debate will focus on a mechanism for  member states to participate  some countries have reservations about  whether an eu level fund covering all 27  nations is the best way to help and  differ over the form that the scheme  should take
 

